<h2>Two streams, two machineries</h2>
<p>Matrimonial money obligations reach courts in two distinct forms, and conflating them is the commonest error in this field. Monthly maintenance under Section 144 BNSS (formerly Section 125 CrPC) has its own summary recovery route: on default, the Magistrate may issue a warrant for levying the amount as a fine, and may sentence the defaulter to imprisonment up to one month for each month&rsquo;s unpaid allowance, on an application made within the statutory window. Decretal amounts — permanent alimony under Section 25 HMA, lump sums in consent decrees, costs — travel the civil route: an execution petition, attachment, sale, and in defined circumstances arrest. The first route is faster but limited to the monthly allowance; the second is slower but reaches the defaulter&rsquo;s entire estate.</p>

<h2>The civil execution toolkit</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>Attachment of salary.</strong> For an employed judgment-debtor, attachment of salary through the employer under Order XXI is the workhorse remedy. Section 60 CPC exempts a slice of salary from attachment, but the exemption is narrowed where the decree is one for maintenance — a deliberate legislative tilt in favour of dependants.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Attachment of accounts and receivables.</strong> Bank accounts, fixed deposits, rent and business receivables can be attached by prohibitory order. The decree-holder&rsquo;s real task is intelligence: execution courts attach what is identified to them, and the affidavits of assets filed during the main case are a ready map.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Attachment and sale of property.</strong> Immovable property can be attached and sold, subject to the exemptions in Section 60. The process is slower — proclamation, objections, sale, confirmation — but against an asset-rich, income-opaque debtor it is often the only remedy with teeth.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Arrest and civil detention.</strong> Sections 51 and 58 CPC permit detention in civil prison where the court records that the debtor has had the means to pay since the decree and has refused or neglected to pay. The thresholds and time limits are strict, and courts use the power sparingly — but the realistic prospect of it concentrates minds remarkably.</div>
</div>

<h2>The sequence that works</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Compute and demand.</strong> Prepare a precise statement of the decretal amount, credits for payments made, and interest where the decree carries it. A short demand notice sometimes produces payment and always produces evidence of wilful default.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>File execution where the assets are.</strong> Under Section 18 of the Family Courts Act the decree is executable by the Family Court itself or by the court where the debtor resides or holds property. Choose the forum nearest the assets, seeking transfer of the decree where needed.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Disclose and discover assets.</strong> Annex the debtor&rsquo;s own asset affidavit from the main proceedings, and seek examination of the judgment-debtor as to means where the picture is stale. Execution courts can summon the debtor and require disclosure on oath.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Escalate deliberately.</strong> Begin with salary and account attachment, move to property, and reserve arrest for demonstrated, wilful default. A record of graduated, reasonable steps also strengthens any parallel contempt plea.</div>
</div>

<h2>Consent decrees and undertakings</h2>
<p>Mutual-consent divorces routinely embody a settlement: a lump sum payable in tranches, often tied to the second motion, with undertakings recorded before the court. Two consequences follow. First, the settlement merges into a decree and is executable as such — the payee need not sue afresh on the agreement. Second, where a party gave a specific undertaking to the court and wilfully breaches it, the breach can, in appropriate cases, be pursued as civil contempt in addition to execution. Courts treat the contempt jurisdiction as a complement to execution, not a substitute for it, and expect the decree-holder to pursue the ordinary remedy in parallel; but the combined pressure of attachment and a contempt notice resolves most defaults without a sale ever being held.</p>

<h2>Common defences, and their real worth</h2>
<div class="check">
  <p>&ldquo;Circumstances have changed.&rdquo; A plea of changed circumstances justifies an application to vary a maintenance order where the statute permits; it is not a defence to execution of a crystallised lump-sum decree.</p>
  <p>&ldquo;Payments were made in cash.&rdquo; Unproved cash payments are routinely rejected. Payers should pay traceably; payees should reconcile credits candidly, since inflated arrears claims damage credibility.</p>
  <p>&ldquo;The property is in a relative&rsquo;s name.&rdquo; Benami-style shielding complicates execution but does not end it; the debtor&rsquo;s examination as to means, and attachment of what is provably his, remain available.</p>
  <p>&ldquo;The decree is old.&rdquo; Article 136 of the Limitation Act allows twelve years. Delay within that window may cost interest and convenience, but not the remedy itself.</p>
</div>

<div class="note">Execution is the stage at which careful work in the main case pays its dividend: the asset affidavits, the disclosed bank accounts, the recorded undertakings. Parties negotiating settlements should draft with execution in mind — fixed dates, default interest, post-dated security, and undertakings recorded in the decree itself — because the cheapest execution petition is the one that never has to be filed.</div>
